## 4. End-to-End Sequence Lifecycle
\`\`\`mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    actor Rider as Rider Client
    actor Driver as Driver Client
    participant GW as API Gateway
    participant Ingest as Location Ingestion
    participant Redis as Redis (H3 Spatial & Locks)
    participant Dispatch as Dispatch Service
    participant DB as PostgreSQL
    participant Kafka as Kafka (trip.lifecycle)
    participant Billing as Billing Worker

    Driver->>GW: WebSocket: GPS Ping (lat, lng, driverId)
    GW->>Ingest: Stream Ping
    Ingest->>Redis: Update H3 Cell Index (geo:cell:{h3Index})
    
    Rider->>GW: POST /api/v1/rides/request (pickup, dropoff)
    GW->>Dispatch: CreateRideRequest(riderId, pickupCoord)
    Dispatch->>Redis: Query Neighbors (geo:cell:{pickupH3Index})
    Redis-->>Dispatch: List of Eligible Drivers [D1, D2, D3]
    Dispatch->>DB: INSERT INTO trips (status='SEARCHING')
    Dispatch->>GW: Dispatch Offer to Driver D1
    
    Driver->>GW: POST /api/v1/rides/accept (rideId, driverId=D1)
    GW->>Dispatch: AcceptRide(rideId, D1)
    Dispatch->>Redis: AcquireLock(lock:ride:{rideId}, ttl=5s)
    alt Lock Acquired (First Driver)
        Dispatch->>DB: UPDATE trips SET status='ACCEPTED', driverId=D1
        Dispatch->>Kafka: Publish(trip.lifecycle, key=rideId, event=RideAccepted)
        Dispatch->>Redis: ReleaseLock(lock:ride:{rideId})
        Dispatch-->>Driver: 200 OK (Trip Confirmed)
        Dispatch-->>Rider: Push Notification (Driver D1 Assigned)
    else Lock Failed / Already Accepted
        Dispatch-->>Driver: 409 Conflict (Offer Expired or Taken)
    end

    Note over Kafka,Billing: Asynchronous Post-Trip Settlement
    Kafka-)Billing: Consume(RideCompletedEvent)
    Billing->>DB: Capture Payment & Generate Invoice
\`\`\`

## 5. Bulletproof Industry Resilience & Failure Modes
- **Concurrency & Double-Booking Protection:** Strict distributed mutex (`SET NX EX`) ensures exactly one driver claims a ride.
- **Thundering Herd & Location Ingestion Scale:** Location pings are batched or routed via Redis pipeline with sliding TTL (30s) to automatically expire offline drivers.
- **Idempotency Invariant:** Every HTTP mutation and Kafka message carries an `idempotencyKey` stored with a 24-hour TTL.
- **Network Partition & Offline Degradation:** If geospatial index is temporarily degraded, fallback to bounding-box range query in PostgreSQL PostGIS.
- **Poison-Pill Handling:** Failed payment processing routes to `trip.lifecycle.dlq` after 3 exponential backoff attempts with jitter.
